@startuml
title Two-phase prepare, sign, send — the protocol behind every Embers mutation
skinparam backgroundColor #F8FBFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor #294C60
skinparam sequence {
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #EAF5FF
  ParticipantBorderColor #38789E
  LifeLineBorderColor #6A92AA
  GroupBackgroundColor #FFF3E8
  GroupBorderColor #D97638
}

actor "Wallet holder" as Holder #FFD8C2
participant "Client (SDK / editor)" as Client #D5EAF7
participant "Embers API" as API #FFC9A8
participant "Domain service" as Domain #CDEACC
participant "F1r3node\nchain + observer" as Chain #FFE4A6

group Phase 1 — prepare (deploys nothing)
Client -> API : POST …/prepare {request R}
API -> Domain : build contract for R
Domain --> API : response C\n(unsigned contract bytes, minted identities)
API -> API : H = h(R, tau_R, C, tau_C)\ntoken = JWT{exp: +1 day, hash: H}
API --> Client : PrepareResponse {response C, token}
end

group Sign — the key never leaves the client
Client -> Holder : show prepared contract
Holder --> Client : signature over contract bytes
Client -> Client : SignedContract\n{contract, sig, sigAlgorithm, deployer}
end

group Phase 2 — send (verify, deploy, read back)
Client -> API : POST …/send\n{prepareRequest R, prepareResponse C,\nrequest: SignedContract, token}
API -> API : decode token; recompute H over echoed R, C
alt hash and expiry verify
  API -> Domain : deploy signed contract
  Domain -> Chain : deploy + await finality
  Chain --> Domain : finalized; observer state
  Domain --> API : deploy id / exact ledger readback
  API --> Client : SendResp or run state
else mismatch, expiry, or restarted backend
  API --> Client : "invalid token or request payloads"\n(prepare again; nothing was deployed)
end
end

note over API #FFF0C7
The token binds send to one prepare exchange:
<latex>H = h(R \parallel \tau_R \parallel C \parallel \tau_C)</latex>
where <latex>\tau_R, \tau_C</latex> are the request and response
type identities. An edited echo cannot verify.
end note

note over Chain #FFF0C7
The chain independently verifies the deployer
signature, so the HTTP layer is never the
trust root for authorship.
end note
@enduml
